---

<BaseLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbItems} />
      <h1>What Is a Bust in Darts? Rules, Penalties & Score Reset Explained</h1>
      <p class="lead-text">
        At the climax of a 501 leg, a single misplaced millimeter can trigger the most demoralizing call in darts: "Bust!" Understand official DRA rules, the mathematical reason why leaving 1 is illegal, score reset mechanics, and tactical bust scenarios.
      </p>
      <div class="meta-row">
        <VerificationBadge />
        <span class="meta-item">Official Standard: DRA Rule 5.4 & PDC Regulations</span>
        <span class="meta-item">Read Time: 6 mins</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container article-layout">
    <main class="main-content">
      <!-- Section: Definition -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>What Does a "Bust" Mean in Darts? (Official DRA Definition)</h2>
        <p>
          In professional darts, every game of 501 is played under strict <strong>"Double-Out"</strong> rules. To win a leg, a player must reduce their running score to exactly zero, with the decisive final dart landing in an outer double segment or the 50-point red inner bullseye.
        </p>
        <p>
          A <strong>bust</strong> is an invalid visit where a player's thrown dart renders the completion of the leg legally or mathematically impossible. Under official Darts Regulation Authority (DRA) Section 5.4, a visit is immediately declared <strong>"Bust"</strong> (or "No Score"), concluding the thrower's turn on the spot.
        </p>

        <div class="callout-box">
          <div class="callout-title">The Three Consequences of a Bust</div>
          <p>
            1. <strong>Immediate Turn Termination:</strong> Your turn ends the instant the busting dart impacts the board.<br />
            2. <strong>Remaining Darts Forfeited:</strong> If you bust on dart 1 or dart 2, you cannot throw any remaining darts in your hand.<br />
            3. <strong>Score Reset:</strong> Your score reverts completely to what it was at the beginning of that visit.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <AdSlot position="in-article" />

      <!-- Section: The 3 Bust Conditions -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>The Three Official Bust Conditions in 501</h2>
        <p>
          Under DRA tournament regulations, a bust occurs under exactly three distinct mathematical scenarios:
        </p>

        <div class="rules-step-list">
          <div class="rule-step">
            <span class="step-num">1</span>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Condition 1: Over-Scoring (Reducing Score Below Zero)</h3>
              <p>
                A player scores more points than their remaining score required. For example, if a player has <strong>32 remaining</strong> and their dart enters <strong>Double 20 (40 points)</strong> or <strong>Treble 20 (60 points)</strong>, their score has dropped below zero (-8 or -28). Because negative scores do not exist in darts, the visit is declared bust.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="rule-step">
            <span class="step-num">2</span>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Condition 2: Leaving Exactly One Point Remaining</h3>
              <p>
                A player throws a dart that reduces their remaining total to <strong>exactly 1</strong>. For example, if a player is on <strong>20</strong> and throws into the <strong>Single 19</strong> bed, they leave a remaining total of 1. Because no legal double on a dartboard equals 1, this triggers an immediate automatic bust.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="rule-step">
            <span class="step-num">3</span>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Condition 3: Reaching Zero Without Finishing on a Legal Double</h3>
              <p>
                A player reduces their score to exactly zero, but the final dart lands in a <strong>single segment</strong> or <strong>treble segment</strong> rather than an outer double or the 50-point red inner bullseye. For example, if a player requires <strong>16 points</strong> and hits the <strong>Single 16</strong> bed, their remaining score is 0, but because the shot was not a double, it is declared bust.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: Why Leaving 1 is Impossible -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>The Mathematical Proof: Why Leaving 1 Is Always a Bust</h2>
        <p>
          Newcomers to darts often ask: <em>"Why can't I just score 1 point, leave zero, and win?"</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          The answer lies in the fundamental geometry and arithmetic of the standard English dartboard under double-out rules:
        </p>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <strong>Target Double Score = 2 &times; N</strong> (where N is any board segment 1 to 20, or 25 for Inner Bull)
        </div>
        <p>
          To finish legally, your remaining score must equal $2 \times N$, where $N$ is the segment face value:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>The lowest double on the board is <strong>Double 1 ($2 \times 1 = 2$)</strong>.</li>
          <li>For a double to equal 1, the face value would have to be: $2 \times N = 1 \implies N = 0.5$.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Because there is no "half-point" bed on a dartboard, it is geometrically and mathematically impossible to finish a leg from a score of 1. Therefore, leaving 1 is classified in the rules as an insurmountable dead-end, triggering an automatic bust and resetting the score.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is closely related to <a href="/darts/bogey-numbers-in-darts/">bogey numbers in darts</a> (such as 169, 168, and 166), which represent remaining totals that cannot be mathematically cleared within a three-dart visit.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: Turn Penalties & Score Reset -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>What Happens to Remaining Darts & Turn Penalties?</h2>
        <p>
          Understanding the sequence of events during a bust clarifies why precision under pressure is paramount on the professional stage.
        </p>

        <h3>Immediate Visit Termination</h3>
        <p>
          Under DRA Rule 5.4.1, a visit ends the instant a bust occurs:
        </p>
        <blockquote class="rules-quote">
          "If a player scores more points than the number required, or scores a number that leaves the score at one, or scores the exact number required without finishing on a double or bullseye, the throw is completed and no points are scored for that throw."
          <footer>— Darts Regulation Authority (DRA) Official Rulebook</footer>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          If you throw dart one into Treble 20 when needing 40, you are <strong>not allowed to practice or throw darts two and three</strong>. You must step up to the board immediately and retrieve your darts.
        </p>

        <h3>Score Reset to Turn Starting Total</h3>
        <p>
          All points landed before the busting dart in that visit are completely erased:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Start of Turn:</strong> You have 60 points remaining.</li>
          <li><strong>Dart 1:</strong> Lands in Single 20 (leaving 40).</li>
          <li><strong>Dart 2:</strong> Aims for Double 20, but drifts into Treble 20 (60 points scored).</li>
          <li><strong>Result:</strong> Bust! Your score does not become 40, nor does it become -20. Your score resets to <strong>60 points</strong> for your subsequent visit.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Referee Calls & Scoring Board Notation</h3>
        <p>
          In televised PDC matches, referees like Russ Bray or George Noble signal a bust by loudly announcing <strong>"Bust!"</strong> or calling <strong>"No score!"</strong>. On electronic tournament scoreboards, the player's turn score is entered as <strong>0</strong>, preserving their beginning total.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: Three Real Match Scenarios -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>Three Real-Match Bust Scenarios Analyzed</h2>
        <p>
          Let us examine how busts manifest during high-pressure tournament legs:
        </p>

        <div class="scenarios-grid">
          <div class="scenario-card">
            <div class="scenario-header">Scenario A: The Treble Overshoot</div>
            <div class="scenario-body">
              <p><strong>Remaining Score:</strong> 36 (Double 18)</p>
              <p><strong>Dart 1:</strong> Aims at Double 18, but misses high and inside, accidentally striking <strong>Treble 18 (54 points)</strong>.</p>
              <p><strong>Score Deducted:</strong> $36 - 54 = -18$ (Below zero).</p>
              <div class="scenario-outcome bust">Outcome: BUST! Score resets to 36. Darts 2 and 3 forfeited.</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="scenario-card">
            <div class="scenario-header">Scenario B: The Single 1 Trap</div>
            <div class="scenario-body">
              <p><strong>Remaining Score:</strong> 40 (Double 20)</p>
              <p><strong>Dart 1:</strong> Lands in Single 1 (leaves 39).</p>
              <p><strong>Dart 2:</strong> Aims at Single 7 to leave Double 16, but slips into <strong>Single 19 (leaves 20)</strong>. Wait—if they instead aimed for 19 and hit <strong>Single 20</strong>, they leave 19.</p>
              <p><strong>Alternative:</strong> On score 32, dart hits Single 15, leaving 17. Second dart aims at Single 1 to leave 16, but hits <strong>Single 16</strong>, leaving <strong>1 point</strong>.</p>
              <div class="scenario-outcome bust">Outcome: BUST! Score resets to 32. Cannot finish on 1.</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="scenario-card">
            <div class="scenario-header">Scenario C: Missing the Double on the Wire</div>
            <div class="scenario-body">
              <p><strong>Remaining Score:</strong> 10 (Double 5)</p>
              <p><strong>Dart 1:</strong> Aims at Double 5, but lands just below the wire into the large <strong>Single 10 bed</strong>.</p>
              <p><strong>Score Deducted:</strong> $10 - 10 = 0$ points remaining.</p>
              <div class="scenario-outcome bust">Outcome: BUST! Reduced to 0 without a double. Score resets to 10.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: Tactical Bust -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>Tactical Bust Situations: Can You Intentionally Bust in Darts?</h2>
        <p>
          While busting is almost always an error, top professionals occasionally utilize a <strong>tactical bust</strong> when game-state mathematics turn against them.
        </p>

        <h3>When and Why a Player Might Intentionally Bust</h3>
        <p>
          Consider a player on <strong>Double 16 (32 remaining)</strong> whose first dart lands astray in the <strong>Single 16</strong> bed, leaving <strong>16 (Double 8)</strong>. They throw their second dart at Double 8, but miss inside into <strong>Single 8</strong>, leaving <strong>8 (Double 4)</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now suppose a player on 4 has their dart land in <strong>Single 3</strong>, leaving <strong>1 point</strong>—that is an accidental auto-bust. But what if a player on an awkward remaining total (such as having their third dart blocked by preceding barrels) deliberately chooses to throw into the surround or aim at a known bust number to ensure their score resets to an open, favored double for the next leg?
        </p>

        <h3>DRA Regulations on Deliberate Busting & Etiquette</h3>
        <p>
          Under DRA Code, throwing intentionally off-board or at a busting number to preserve a beginning-of-turn score is fully legal, provided:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>The dart is released legitimately from behind the oche toward the target area.</li>
          <li>The player does not violate slow-play or unsporting conduct rules.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          In practice, modern professionals rarely bust intentionally because scoring down to any double (even Double 1 / "Madhouse") is mathematically superior to forfeiting a visit while an opponent sits on a checkout. Check our complete <a href="/darts/checkout-chart/">501 Darts Checkout Chart</a> to explore standard professional setups and escape routes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: FAQ -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About the Bust Rule in Darts</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What is a bust in darts?</h3>
            <p>
              A bust in darts occurs when a player throws a dart that either scores more points than are required to win the leg, leaves a remaining score of exactly one, or reduces their score to zero without landing on a legal double or the inner bullseye.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What happens to your score when you bust in darts?</h3>
            <p>
              When you bust, all points scored during that visit are wiped out. Your score immediately resets to the exact total you had at the beginning of that three-dart turn, your turn ends, and any darts remaining in your hand cannot be thrown.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why can you not leave 1 in darts?</h3>
            <p>
              You cannot leave 1 because standard 501 darts requires finishing on a double. The lowest possible double on a dartboard is Double 1, which equals 2 points. Because no whole-number double equals 1, leaving 1 makes completing the leg mathematically impossible, triggering an automatic bust.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Can you throw remaining darts after busting?</h3>
            <p>
              No. Under official Darts Regulation Authority (DRA) Rule 5.4, the moment a bust occurs, the player's turn is officially over. Any darts remaining in the player's hand must be retained and cannot be thrown at the board.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Is a tactical bust allowed in darts?</h3>
            <p>
              Yes. A player may legally choose to bust intentionally—such as aiming off-target or hitting a known bust number—to preserve a preferred double setup for their following turn, provided all darts are thrown from behind the oche toward the board.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: Related Guides -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>Related Darts Rules & Checkout Resources</h2>
        <div class="links-grid">
          <a href="/darts/rules/" class="link-card">
            <strong>Official 501 Darts Rules</strong>
            <span>Complete guide to double-out rules, board heights, oche measurements, and scoring.</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/darts/checkout-chart/" class="link-card">
            <strong>501 Darts Checkout Chart</strong>
            <span>Master 2-dart and 3-dart finishing combinations from 170 down to double 1.</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/darts/bogey-numbers-in-darts/" class="link-card">
            <strong>Bogey Numbers in Darts</strong>
            <span>Explore the 7 impossible checkouts below 170 and why 169 cannot be finished.</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/darts/why-is-170-the-highest-checkout/" class="link-card">
            <strong>Why 170 Is the Highest Checkout</strong>
            <span>The mathematical proof of "The Big Fish" and maximum 3-dart checkout limits.</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/darts/what-is-a-180-in-darts/" class="link-card">
            <strong>What Is a 180 in Darts?</strong>
            <span>Why three Treble 20s form the maximum score and how it beats three bullseyes.</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/darts/board-height-and-distance/" class="link-card">
            <strong>Dartboard Height & Distance</strong>
            <span>Regulation setup dimensions, diagonal verification, and home installation guide.</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <aside class="sidebar">
      <div class="sidebar-card">
        <h3>Bust Rule Quick Summary</h3>
        <ul class="fact-list">
          <li><strong>Over-Scoring:</strong> Dropping below 0 = Bust</li>
          <li><strong>Leaving 1:</strong> Mathematically impossible = Bust</li>
          <li><strong>Non-Double 0:</strong> Reaching 0 on single/treble = Bust</li>
          <li><strong>Score Consequence:</strong> Resets to turn starting total</li>
          <li><strong>Darts in Hand:</strong> Forfeited immediately</li>
          <li><strong>Referee Call:</strong> "Bust" or "No Score"</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="sidebar-card">
        <h3>Official Rules Standard</h3>
        <p class="final-highlight">
          Administered under Darts Regulation Authority (DRA) Rule 5.4 (Scoring and Completion of Legs).
        </p>
        <a href="/darts/rules/" class="btn btn-secondary full-width">Read Full Rules Guide</a>
      </div>

      <AdSlot position="sidebar" />
    </aside>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

